WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.120
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:11.240
Today we are going to see that some computer
graphics simulation techniques are so accurate,

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:16.630
they can come out to the real world, and even
teach us something new.

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:18.939
And it can do this too.

00:00:18.939 --> 00:00:21.329
And, this too.

00:00:21.329 --> 00:00:26.819
Today, computer graphics research techniques
are capable of conjuring up these beautiful

00:00:26.819 --> 00:00:32.050
virtual worlds where we can engage in the
favorite pastime of the computer graphics

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:37.270
researcher…which is destroying them in a
spectacular manner.

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:42.870
Here you see Joshuah Wolper’s paper, who
is a returning guest in the series.

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:49.420
He was a PhD student at the time and his first
work we showcased was about breaking bread,

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:54.530
and visualizing all the damage that takes
place during this process.

00:00:54.530 --> 00:01:02.120
His later work was about enriching our simulations
with anisotropic damage and elasticity.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:04.320
So what does that mean exactly?

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:11.130
This means that it supports more extreme topological
changes in these virtual objects.

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:13.630
In the meantime, he has graduated.

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:18.549
So, congratulations on all the amazing works
Dr. Joshuah Wolper!

00:01:18.549 --> 00:01:23.369
And let’s see what he has been up to since
he leveled up.

00:01:23.369 --> 00:01:30.329
Note that all these previous works are about
simulating fracturing and damage.

00:01:30.329 --> 00:01:35.289
I wonder what else could all this knowledge
be applied for?

00:01:35.289 --> 00:01:39.340
Hmm…how about simulating glacier fracture?

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:41.119
Yes, really!

00:01:41.119 --> 00:01:45.090
But before we start, why would we do that?

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:52.179
Because a technique like this could help assess
and identify potential hazards ahead of time,

00:01:52.179 --> 00:01:56.009
and get this, maybe even mitigate them.

00:01:56.009 --> 00:02:02.530
Who knows, maybe we could even go full Hari
Seldon and predict potential hazards before

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:03.719
they happen.

00:02:03.719 --> 00:02:05.329
Let’s see how.

00:02:05.329 --> 00:02:07.950
To start out, we need three things.

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:11.060
First, we need to simulate ice fracturing.

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:16.730
Here is a related earlier work, however, this
is on snow.

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:18.500
Ice is different.

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:20.660
That is going to be a challenge.

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:28.810
Two, we need to simulate the ocean, and three,
simulate how the two react to each other.

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:35.190
Wow, that is going to be quite a challenge,
because capturing all of these really accurately

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:38.910
requires multiple different algorithms.

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:44.680
You may remember from this previous work how
difficult it is to marry two simulation algorithms.

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:53.960
Believe it or not, this is not one, but two
simulations, one inside the box, and one outside.

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:59.770
To make all this happen, plenty of work had
to be done in the transition zones, so this

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:04.370
one for ice fractures might be even more challenging.

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:09.890
And you may rest assured that we will not
let this paper go until we also see my favorite

00:03:09.890 --> 00:03:16.060
thing in all simulation research, which is,
of course, comparing the simulation results

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:17.490
to real-world footage.

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:23.000
For instance, the results would have to agree
with this earlier lab experiment by Heller

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:30.060
and colleagues that measures how the ocean
reacts to a huge block of ice falling into

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:31.060
it.

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:33.650
Now, hold on for a second.

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:37.460
We can’t just say that it “falls” into
the ocean.

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:41.220
There are multiple kinds of falling into the
ocean.

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:53.680
For instance, it can either happen due to
gravity or to buoyancy, or capsizing.

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:55.870
So we have two questions.

00:03:55.870 --> 00:03:58.860
Question number one: does this matter?

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:01.410
Well, let’s have a look.

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:06.010
Oh, yes, it does matter a great deal.

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:09.040
The generated waves look quite different.

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:15.430
Now, here comes the most exciting part, question
number two: do Dr. Wolper’s simulations

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:18.750
agree with this real lab experiment?

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:21.690
To start out, we wish to see three experiments.

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:28.970
One for gravity, the color coding goes from
colder to warmer colors as the velocity of

00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:31.260
the waves increases.

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:39.430
We also have one simulation for buoyancy,
and one for capsizing.

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:45.030
We could say they look excellent, but we can’t
say that because we don’t yet know how this

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:49.020
experiment relates to the lab experiment yet.

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:54.340
Before we compare the two, let’s also add
one more variable: theory.

00:04:54.340 --> 00:05:00.570
We expect that that simulations match the
theory nearly perfectly, and to more or less

00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:03.280
match the lab experiment.

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:07.400
Why only more or less, why not perfectly?

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:12.940
Because it is hard to reproduce the exact
forces, geometries and materials that were

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:14.810
used in the experiment.

00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:20.960
Now, let’s see…the solid lines follow
the dashed line very well.

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:24.650
This means that the simulation follows the
theory nearly perfectly.

00:05:24.650 --> 00:05:31.540
For the simulation, this plot is a little
easier to read, and shows that the lab experiment

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:34.440
is within the error limits of the simulation.

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:41.139
Now, at this point, yes, it is justified to
say - this, is an excellent work.

00:05:41.139 --> 00:05:48.310
Now, let’s ramp up the complexity of these
simulations and hopefully give it a hard time.

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:49.430
Look!

00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:51.100
Now we’re talking!

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:53.610
Real icebergs, real calving.

00:05:53.610 --> 00:06:00.270
The paper also shows plots that compare this
experiment to theoretical results, and found

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:02.940
good agreement there too.

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:03.940
Very good.

00:06:03.940 --> 00:06:09.710
Now if it can deal with this, hold on to your
papers, and let’s bring forth the final

00:06:09.710 --> 00:06:10.710
boss.

00:06:10.710 --> 00:06:12.230
Eqip Sermia.

00:06:12.230 --> 00:06:14.740
Well…what is that?

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:22.010
This was a real glacier fracturing event in
Greenland that involved 800 thousand metric

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:24.190
tons of ice.

00:06:24.190 --> 00:06:27.500
And at this point I said, I am out.

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:34.220
There are just too many variables, too many
unknowns, too complex a situation to get meaningful

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:35.220
results.

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:38.080
800 thousand metric tons?

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:42.130
You can’t possibly reproduce this with a
simulation.

00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:48.341
Well, hold on to your papers, and watch this.

00:06:48.341 --> 00:06:56.040
This is the reproduction, and even better,
we have measured data about wave amplitudes,

00:06:56.040 --> 00:07:03.150
average wave speed, iceberg sizes involved
in this event, and get this, this simulation

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:06.890
is able to reproduce all of these accurately.

00:07:06.890 --> 00:07:08.110
Wow.

00:07:08.110 --> 00:07:11.040
And, we are still not done yet!

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:17.580
It can also produce full 3D simulations, which
requires the interplay of tens of millions

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:21.151
of particles, and can create beautiful footage
like this.

00:07:21.151 --> 00:07:26.430
This is not only beautiful, but useful too.

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:27.430
Look!

00:07:27.430 --> 00:07:32.729
We can even assemble a scene that reenacts
what would happen if we were sitting in a

00:07:32.729 --> 00:07:34.560
boat nearby.

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:36.960
Spoiler alert: it’s not fun.

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:42.820
So, there we go, some of these computer graphics
simulations are so accurate, they can come

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:47.389
out to the real world, and even teach us new
things.

00:07:47.389 --> 00:07:53.860
Reading papers makes me very, very happy,
and this was no exception - I had a fantastic

00:07:53.860 --> 00:07:56.010
time reading this paper.

00:07:56.010 --> 00:08:50.020
If you wish to have a great time too, make
sure to check it out in the video description.

00:08:50.020 --> 00:08:52.710
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

